A CONTINUATION PARAGRAPH IS TORN OUT OF ITS ITEM BY A NESTED LIST.

WHAT md DOES TODAY. The tail paragraph comes back as a top-level bullet of
its own:

    - [x] Parent task

        - Nested note

    - Tail paragraph of the parent.      <-- invented bullet

    - [ ] Sibling task

WHAT IT SHOULD DO. The tail was authored as a continuation paragraph inside
the first item, so it stays there, indented under the item content:

      Tail paragraph of the parent.

WHY THIS IS A MEANING DEFECT AND NOT A FORMATTING ONE. The author wrote TWO
top-level items. The document now says there are THREE, and the third one is
prose that was never a list item at all. On a task list that reads as a task
nobody wrote. Every edit by index then lands one item out, because `replace`,
`remove` and `insert-after` all count what the block model reports; see
`blocks-counts-an-item-split-by-a-nested-list-once`.

md ALREADY AGREES WITH THIS EXPECTATION when no nested list is involved.
`blocks-counts-a-task-item-continuation-inside-its-item` passes today and
asserts exactly the same principle: "A continuation paragraph belongs to the
item above it, so a task list of two items with a continuation is TWO items
and not three." The nested list is the only thing that changes the answer, and
it has no business changing it.

IT DAMAGES A CORRECT DOCUMENT. Feed `expected.md` here back to `md format`
and the tail is torn out again. So this is not merely a shape md declines to
produce; it is a shape md destroys. A document that was right before the run
is wrong after it.

CAUSE. `MarkdownParser.collectListItems`. `MarkdownBlock.list` holds ONE FLAT
array of items, so an item cannot hold "text, then a nested list, then more
text". The code copes by flushing the gathered text as a separate `ListItem`
each time it meets a nested list, and the piece after the list becomes a
sibling item indistinguishable from one the author wrote.

The flat model is the real constraint. Note that the multi-paragraph case
already works: two paragraphs with no nested list between them are joined into
one `ListItem.text` with a blank line, and `BlockFormatter` writes the second
at the continuation indent. Only the ordering forced by a nested list defeats
that, because the nested items must be written BETWEEN the two paragraphs.

A FIX THAT FITS THE MODEL. Mark the pieces flushed after the first as
continuations of the item above rather than items in their own right: a
`continuation` flag on `ListItem`, set for every piece after the first flush
of one item. `BlockFormatter` writes a continuation at the parent's
continuation indent with no marker and no checkbox, and whatever counts items
skips it. That leaves the flat array intact and needs no change to the arity
of `MarkdownBlock.list`.

DO NOT let this case's fix put a checkbox back on the tail. See
`format-does-not-invent-a-checkbox-on-a-split-item`, which guards that and
whose expected bytes change when this defect is fixed.
